%[text] # OPT 212: Numerical Derivatives Coding Exercises
%[text] Student version
%[text] These exercises accompany the Numerical Derivatives active script. They focus on finite differences, error behavior, gradients, and surface normals for optical surfaces.
%[text] Complete the TODO blocks in each work area. The companion solution file is opt212\_03\_numerical\_derivatives\_solutions.m.
clear
close all
clc
format compact
set(groot,'DefaultAxesFontSize',14,'DefaultTextFontSize',14, ...
    'DefaultLegendFontSize',14,'DefaultColorbarFontSize',14);
%%
%[text] ## Exercise 1: Finite-Difference Derivatives of a Wave
%[text] Consider
%[text]{"align":"center"} $f(x)=\\cos(kx),\\qquad {df\\over dx}=-k\\sin(kx).$
%[text] **1a Tasks**
%[text] 1. Use a wavelength of 633 nm. Convert it to micrometers before computing $k=2\\pi/\\lambda$ so it is compatible with `x` and `h`.
%[text] 2.  Evaluate forward and centered finite-difference derivatives at $x=\\lambda/8$ for many total sample separations `h`.
%[text] 3.  For the centered derivative, use \
%[text]{"align":"center"} $f'(x)\\approx {f(x+h/2)-f(x-h/2)\\over h}.$
%[text]           4\. Compare both approximations with the analytic derivative. The exact derivative is
%[text]{"align":"center"} $f'(x)=-k\\sin(kx).$
%[text]           5\. Make a log-log plot of absolute error versus total sample separation.
%[text] **1b Tasks**
%[text] 1. Use the value of h in the exercise above that gave the minimum error and compare the derivative of the sinc function $f(x)=\\sin(\\pi x)/(\\pi x)$ using the forward and centered difference methods.  Display the function and its derivative in one plot over the range x = -5 to 5.   Display the difference between the forward and centered difference methods in a second plot.   \
%[text] ## Exercise 1a Work Area
%[text] Add your code for Exercise 1 here.  The result should look something like this:
%[text] ![](text:image:4bc3)
% Define the fixed parameters
lambda = 633e-3;      
k = 2*pi/lambda;   

% Define the function and its derivative
f = @(x)    cos(k*x);
df = @(x)   -k*sin(k*x);

% Set the value of x
x = lambda/8 %[output:4cce7d85]

% Sample 101 values for h from 1e-14 to 1e-2 using the logspace command
h = logspace(-14, -2, 101);

% Evaluate the exact derivative
exact = df(x);

% Evaluate the forward difference derivative
forward = (f(x + h) - f(x)) ./ h %[output:9db98523]

% Evaluate the centered* derivative
centered = (f(x + h/2) - f(x - h/2)) ./ h;

% Calculate and plot the error for each of the derivatives
forward_error  = abs(forward  - exact);
centered_error = abs(centered - exact);

figure %[output:1523dd88]
loglog(h,forward_error,'-r','LineWidth',2) %[output:1523dd88]
xlabel('h') %[output:1523dd88]
ylabel('Error in Derivative') %[output:1523dd88]
hold on %[output:1523dd88]
loglog(h,centered_error,'-b','LineWidth',2) %[output:1523dd88]
%[text] ## Exercise 1b Work Area
%[text] The results of this should look something like the following picture:
%[text] ![](text:image:5a5c)
% Put your code here
xs  = linspace(-5, 5, 101);          
g   = @(x) sin(pi*x) ./ (pi*x);
dg  = @(x) (pi*x.*cos(pi*x) - sin(pi*x)) ./ (pi*x.^2); 

forward_s = (g(xs + h) - g(xs)) ./ h;
centered_s = (g(xs + h/2) - g(xs - h/2)) ./ h;

figure %[output:2583a99d]
plot(xs, g(xs), 'k', 'LineWidth', 2); hold on %[output:2583a99d]
plot(xs, dg(xs), 'g', 'LineWidth', 2) %[output:2583a99d]
plot(xs, forward_s, '--r', 'LineWidth', 1.5) %[output:2583a99d]
plot(xs, centered_s, ':b', 'LineWidth', 2) %[output:2583a99d]
xlabel('x'); ylabel('sinc(x) and derivative') %[output:2583a99d]
legend('sinc(x)', 'Exact derivative', 'Forward diff', 'Centered diff') %[output:2583a99d]
grid on %[output:2583a99d]
figure %[output:716eb799]
plot(xs, forward_s, '--r', 'LineWidth', 1.5); hold on %[output:716eb799]
plot(xs, centered_s, ':b', 'LineWidth', 2) %[output:716eb799]
grid on %[output:716eb799]
%[text] ## 
%[text] ## Exercise 2: Gradient of a Gaussian Intensity Pattern
%[text] A transverse beam irradiance can be modeled by
%[text]{"align":"center"} $I(x,y)=I\_0\\exp\\left\[-2{x^2+y^2\\over w^2}\\right\].$
%[text] Use a beam waist `w = 1.2 mm`, peak irradiance `I0 = 1` in arbitrary units, and a transverse grid from -3 mm to +3 mm.
%[text] **Tasks 2a:**
%[text] 1\. Sample the irradiance on a two-dimensional grid using mm for both coordinate axes. 
%[text] 2\. Calculate the partial derivatives to estimate $\\partial I/\\partial x$ and $\\partial I/\\partial y$ using h=1e-5 and the centered difference method.   
%[text] 3\. Plot the irradiance as a contour map and overlay a sparse gradient vector field. 
%[text] **Task 2b**
%[text] Carry out the same exercise for a Hermite-Gauss intensity pattern:  
%[text]{"align":"center"} $I(x,y)=I\_0  {x^2 \\over w^2}\\exp\\left\[-2{x^2+y^2\\over w^2}\\right\].$
%[text] ## Exercise 2 Work Area
%[text] TODO: Add your code for Exercise 2 here. The result should look something like this:
%[text] ![](text:image:791d)
% Define beam parameters
waist_mm = 1.2  ; 
I0 =  1      ;
x = linspace(-2,2,121);
y = linspace(-2,2,121);
[X,Y] = meshgrid(x,y);  % Define a meshgrid for X and Y

% Set h for the derivative calculation
h = 1e-5;
 
% Define the irradiance function
Irr = @(X,Y) I0.*exp(-2*(X.^2 + Y.^2)/waist_mm^2);  

% Compute the derivatives using centered difference
dIdx = (Irr(X + h/2, Y) - Irr(X - h/2, Y)) / h; 
dIdy = (Irr(X, Y + h/2) - Irr(X, Y - h/2)) / h;

% Plot the contours of the beam and show the gradient
figure %[output:197ec4a5]
contourf(X, Y, Irr(X,Y), 15) %[output:197ec4a5]
hold on %[output:197ec4a5]

% Set up a sparse array that samples only every 8 points 
idx = 1:8:numel(x);

% Create a quiver plot that plots gradient arrows every 8th point
quiver(X(idx,idx), Y(idx,idx), dIdx(idx,idx), dIdy(idx,idx), 'k') %[output:197ec4a5]

hold off %[output:197ec4a5]
% % Set up and label axes
axis equal tight %[output:197ec4a5]
colorbar %[output:197ec4a5]
xlabel('x (mm)') %[output:197ec4a5]
ylabel('y (mm)') %[output:197ec4a5]
% title('Gradient of Gaussian beam');
%[text] ## Exercise 2b Work Area
%[text] The results of this should look something like this:
%[text] ![](text:image:7704)
% Define beam parameters
waist_mm = 1.2  ; 
I0 =  1      ;
x = linspace(-2,2,121);
y = linspace(-2,2,121);
[X,Y] = meshgrid(x,y);  % Define a meshgrid for X and Y

% Set h for the derivative calculation
h = 1e-5;
 
% Define the irradiance function
Irr = @(X,Y) I0*(X.^2/waist_mm^2).*exp(-2*(X.^2 + Y.^2)/waist_mm^2);  

% Compute the derivatives using centered difference
dIdx = (Irr(X + h/2, Y) - Irr(X - h/2, Y))/h; 
dIdy = (Irr(X, Y + h/2) - Irr(X, Y - h/2))/h;


% Plot the contours of the beam and show the gradient
figure %[output:771220e6]
contourf(X, Y, Irr(X,Y), 15) %[output:771220e6]
hold on %[output:771220e6]

% Set up a sparse array that samples only every 8 points 
idx = 1:8:numel(x);

% Create a quiver plot that plots gradient arrows every 8th point
quiver(X(idx,idx), Y(idx,idx), dIdx(idx,idx), dIdy(idx,idx), 'k') %[output:771220e6]

hold off %[output:771220e6]
% % Set up and label axes
axis equal tight %[output:771220e6]
colorbar %[output:771220e6]
xlabel('x (mm)') %[output:771220e6]
ylabel('y (mm)') %[output:771220e6]

